# Target C-II — the modern high-`t` ledger forces a linear population of bounded pentagonal contact faces

**Date:** 2026-09-15  
**Status:** proof-only source-level structural strengthening  
**Scope:** canonical full-core/no-macroscopic-container Type-II branch after the hostile-audited `t=(q-k)/D<=1.844` exclusion; quantitative staircase stated through `t<=1.860`  
**Dependencies:** hostile-audited modern high-`t` train-slack theorem; canonical Sections 63, 67, 80, and 85  
**Certificate:** `research/verify_target_c_high_t_pentagon_face_floor.py`  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** `STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.`

The modern high-`t` chamber theorem proves that every remaining full-core survivor has
linear train-boundary slack

\[
\liminf {\tau_D\over D}\ge \rho+0.007707,
\qquad \rho=\cos\rho=0.7390851332\ldots,
\tag{PF.1}
\]

where

\[
\tau_D=(q-k)+2f-2\sqrt{R_4}.
\tag{PF.2}
\]

Here `f` is the number of bounded nonquadrilateral contact faces and

\[
Q=\sum_P(\deg P-4)
\tag{PF.3}
\]

is their total degree-above-four defect.

The previous chamber notes used this currency to force large total inactive-interface
and Oler surplus.  In the live quantitative high-`t` windows there is an additional
discrete consequence: the same upper bound on

\[
t={q-k\over D}
\]

that supports the dominant O--O staircase also caps `Q`, because the old outer contact
walk already has asymptotic length at least `pi D`.  The two inequalities force most
of the linearly many exceptional faces to have the **minimum possible degree five**.

Let `P_5` be the number of bounded pentagonal contact faces.  Then the following
staircase holds:

\[
\boxed{
t\le1.850
\Longrightarrow
\liminf {P_5\over D}>0.243,
}
\tag{PF.4}
\]

\[
\boxed{
t\le1.855
\Longrightarrow
\liminf {P_5\over D}>0.228,
}
\tag{PF.5}
\]

and

\[
\boxed{
t\le1.860
\Longrightarrow
\liminf {P_5\over D}>0.213.
}
\tag{PF.6}
\]

At the widest endpoint `1.860`, one also has

\[
\boxed{
\liminf {f\over D}>0.3957,
\qquad
\limsup {Q\over D}<0.5785,
}
\tag{PF.7}
\]

and consequently more than `53%` of the bounded nonquadrilateral faces are pentagons
asymptotically.  The exceptional bounded faces alone pay

\[
\boxed{
\liminf {A_{exc}-a_0 Z_{def}\over D}>0.514,
\qquad a_0={\sqrt3\over2},
}
\tag{PF.8}
\]

before any outer-pocket chamber contribution is used.

Thus the thin-exceptional-face regression in the surviving high-`t` branch is no
longer an unspecified collection of nonquadrilateral chambers.  A macroscopic linear
population consists of actual convex unit-edge pentagons.

No positive width or area cost per pentagon is asserted.  Near-degenerate convex
pentagons remain compatible with the current Section-84 corridor collapse, so this is
not yet a C-II contradiction.

---

## 1. Modern train slack forces many exceptional faces

Use the exact modern identity

\[
\boxed{
\tau_D=(q-k)+2f-2\sqrt{R_4}.
}
\tag{PF.9}
\]

Canonical Type-II saturation gives

\[
{R_4\over D^2}\to
\Delta_2:={\pi\over2\sqrt3}.
\tag{PF.10}
\]

Fix an endpoint `T` and suppose along the survivor sequence

\[
t={q-k\over D}\le T+o(1).
\tag{PF.11}
\]

Rearranging (PF.9), dividing by `D`, and combining (PF.1), (PF.10), and (PF.11) gives

\[
\boxed{
\liminf {f\over D}
\ge
f_0(T):=
{\rho+0.007707-T+2\sqrt{\Delta_2}\over2}.
}
\tag{PF.12}
\]

The exact certificate uses the safe lower bound `rho>0.739085` and outward enclosures
for `pi`, `sqrt(3)`, and `sqrt(Delta_2)`.

At the three useful endpoints it gives

\[
\begin{array}{c|c}
T & f_0(T)\\ \hline
1.850 & >0.4007088\\
1.855 & >0.3982088\\
1.860 & >0.3957088.
\end{array}
\tag{PF.13}

This is substantially stronger, on the live bounded-`t` windows, than the older
proof-only `0.1671D` exceptional-face floor obtained from the pre-audit AG42 endpoint.

---

## 2. The same `t` window gives an upper bound on total degree defect

Canonical loaded-hull algebra gives the exact finite identity

\[
\boxed{b+Q=2(q-k),}
\tag{PF.14}
\]

where `b` is the occurrence-counted boundary length of the old outer contact face.

The image of that closed unit-edge walk contains every extreme vertex of the force
hull `F`; hence, canonically,

\[
\operatorname{per}(F)\le b.
\tag{PF.15}
\]

Type II gives the disk limit

\[
{\operatorname{per}(F)\over D}\to\pi.
\tag{PF.16}
\]

Therefore (PF.14)--(PF.16) imply

\[
\boxed{
\limsup {Q\over D}
\le Q_0(T):=2T-\pi.
}
\tag{PF.17}
\]

The exact outward values used by the certificate are

\[
\begin{array}{c|c}
T & Q_0(T)\\ \hline
1.850 & <0.558408\\
1.855 & <0.568408\\
1.860 & <0.578408.
\end{array}
\tag{PF.18}

The point is the direction of the inequality: a larger outer-face detour only makes
`Q` smaller.  No assumption `b/D->pi` is needed; the lower bound
`b>=per(F)=pi D-o(D)` suffices.

---

## 3. Degree arithmetic forces pentagons

For each bounded nonquadrilateral face `P`, put

\[
r_P:=\deg(P)-4\ge1.
\tag{PF.19}
\]

A pentagon has `r_P=1`, while every face of degree at least six has `r_P>=2`.  Hence

\[
\begin{aligned}
Q
&=\sum_P r_P\\
&\ge P_5+2(f-P_5)\\
&=2f-P_5.
\end{aligned}
\]

Therefore, finitely,

\[
\boxed{P_5\ge2f-Q.}
\tag{PF.20}
\]

Combining (PF.12), (PF.17), and (PF.20),

\[
\boxed{
\liminf {P_5\over D}
\ge
p_5(T):=2f_0(T)-Q_0(T).
}
\tag{PF.21}
\]

The expression simplifies to

\[
\boxed{
p_5(T)
=ho+0.007707+2\sqrt{\Delta_2}+\pi-3T.}
\tag{PF.22}
\]

The exact certificate proves

\[
\begin{array}{c|c}
T & p_5(T)\\ \hline
1.850 & >0.2430102\\
1.855 & >0.2280102\\
1.860 & >0.2130102.
\end{array}
\tag{PF.23}

This proves (PF.4)--(PF.6).

A useful equivalent statement controls the degree distribution.  Since

\[
Q-f=\sum_P(\deg P-5),
\tag{PF.24}
\]

the number of faces of degree at least six is at most `Q-f`.  At `T=1.860`, the
bounds above give

\[
\limsup {\#\{P:\deg P\ge6\}\over D}
<0.18270.
\tag{PF.25}
\]

Meanwhile `f/D>0.39570`, so the pentagonal fraction satisfies

\[
\liminf {P_5\over f}
\ge 2-{Q_0(1.860)\over f_0(1.860)}
>0.5383.
\tag{PF.26}
\]

The certificate records the simpler safe conclusion `>0.53`.  At `T=1.855` and
`1.850`, the corresponding safe fractions are `>0.57` and `>0.60`.

---

## 4. Exceptional bounded faces already carry most of the new chamber surplus

Canonical Section 67 applies Oler face by face.  With `Z_def` the inactive source
population inside bounded nonquadrilateral faces and `A_exc` their total area,

\[
\boxed{
A_{exc}
\ge a_0(Z_{def}+f)+c_0Q,
\qquad
c_0={\sqrt3\over4}.
}
\tag{PF.27}
\]

Thus

\[
A_{exc}-a_0Z_{def}
\ge a_0f+c_0Q.
\tag{PF.28}
\]

Since every exceptional face has degree at least five, `Q>=f`, and therefore

\[
\boxed{
A_{exc}-a_0Z_{def}
\ge (a_0+c_0)f
={3\sqrt3\over4}f.
}
\tag{PF.29}
\]

Combining with (PF.13), the exact certificate gives

\[
\begin{array}{c|c}
T & (A_{exc}-a_0Z_{def})/D\\ \hline
1.850 & >0.520\\
1.855 & >0.517\\
1.860 & >0.514.
\end{array}
\tag{PF.30}

At the widest endpoint, this already accounts for most of the independently proved
modern total chamber-surplus floor

\[
(A_{ch}-a_0Z_{ch})/D\ge0.586223-o(1).
\]

The statement is only a lower-bound comparison.  No upper bound is inferred for the
outer-pocket contribution.

---

## 5. Relation to the existing second-order rhombus deficit

The pentagon floor does not by itself contradict the current linear rhombus-count
deficit.  Indeed the area decomposition gives, after discarding nonnegative terms,

\[
\Delta_2D^2-R_4
\ge f+{Q\over2}-o(D)
\ge {3\over2}f-o(D).
\tag{PF.31}
\]

At `T=1.860`, this only forces a coefficient above `0.5935`, whereas the current
modern Section-85 population package is compatible with a still larger linear deficit.
Thus no scalar `R_4` contradiction is being hidden here.

The gain is discrete geometry: the deficit is carried by a linear population of actual
five-sided convex contact chambers, rather than by an unspecified aggregate degree
currency.

---

## 6. Updated high-`t` normal form

Combine the present theorem with the live high-`t` O--O path/hub results.  Every
survivor in

\[
1.844<t\le1.855
\]

simultaneously has

```text
>0.0184 D low-energy same-phase O--O cuts on one exact dual path;
one common dual region on every actual diameter support interval;
a nonhub adjacent strip with <=341 one-sided moving diameter bars;
exact nonzero strip reaction generated by <=2 of those bars;
>0.228 D bounded pentagonal contact faces;
>0.517 D exceptional-face Oler surplus.
```

At the wider endpoint `t<=1.860`, the path theorem weakens to the existing Helly-hub /
region-saturation normal form, while the present face theorem still gives
`P_5>0.213D-o(D)`.

These are independent structural pressures: the O--O path is selected from the
dominant train orbit, while `P_5` counts global bounded contact faces.

---

## 7. What remains open

A convex unit-edge pentagon can be arbitrarily thin, so neither (PF.6) nor (PF.8)
creates a uniform Euclidean area penalty per face.  This is exactly the Section-84
near-geodesic-corridor regression.

The next useful theorem should exploit information unavailable to generic convex
pentagons.  Two concrete routes are now sharper:

1. **pentagon/train incidence:** prove that a positive fraction of the `>0.228D`
   pentagons in the live path band meet the selected near-antipodal O--O path or its
   adjacent rhombic carriers, and show that the resulting local five-sided transition
   is incompatible with the one-sided diameter-routing hub; or
2. **pentagon phase defect:** show that a near-degenerate unit-edge pentagon separating
   two strict-rhombus phase regions pays a positive amount of the already bounded
   angular/directional defect, giving a global upper bound on the number of such
   pentagons.

No C-II or Level-I closure is claimed.

**Disposition:** proved proof-only high-`t` bounded-pentagon population and
exceptional-face Oler-surplus staircase.  C-II and Level I remain open.
